(eval `(do ,@(source "site.sx")))

(define (stage name file & description)
  (div :margin 14
    (p :margin 3 (b :size 17 name) "  " (code file))
    (p :margin 0 muted description)))

(chapter "internals.sx"
  (p "Netscheme is written in Rust, in about seven thousand lines with its tests. It draws"
     " everything itself, from the text to the caret in the address bar, using a"
     " window, a buffer of pixels, and a font.")

  (section "From source to pixels")
  (p "Showing a page takes six steps, and each is a module of its own:")
  (code-block
"source text --read--> forms --evaluate--> element tree
             --style--> styled boxes --lay out--> display list
             --paint--> pixels")
  (stage "Read" "lisp/reader.rs"
         "Turns the text into values: lists, symbols, strings and numbers. It's"
         " the equivalent of a browser's HTML parser, but it doesn't know about"
         " elements at all.")
  (stage "Evaluate" "lisp/eval.rs"
         "Runs those values as a program. Element functions are ordinary"
         " functions that return elements, so the program's result is the"
         " element tree. This step does the work that a browser's JavaScript"
         " engine and its DOM construction do between them.")
  (stage "Style" "style.rs"
         "Works out each element's colours, font and spacing: first its tag's"
         " defaults, then its attributes, with text properties inherited from its"
         " parent. It's CSS's cascade with only one, very specific, selector.")
  (stage "Lay out" "layout.rs"
         "Stacks blocks, breaks text into words and words into lines, and places"
         " frames, producing a list of rectangles and runs of text to draw.")
  (stage "Paint" "paint.rs"
         "Draws the list into a buffer of pixels, with fontdue rasterizing each"
         " glyph, and images scaled to size and blended by their transparency."
         " After the first frame it only paints what changed: " (code "damage.rs")
         " compares the new list with the last one, and moves the pixels of"
         " whatever scrolled rather than painting them again. The buffer goes to"
         " the window through softbuffer, with the parts that changed marked.")
  (stage "Run the session" "browser.rs, window.rs"
         "Holds the history, the state, scrolling and focus, and turns clicks"
         " and keys into calls on the page.")

  (section "Running again")
  (p "Netscheme keeps no live document between events. When you click a"
     " button, the browser calls its handler, which changes some state, and then"
     " it runs the page again from the top. The new element tree is styled, laid"
     " out and painted from scratch.")
  (p "There's one shortcut. The browser notes which state the page reads as it"
     " runs, and which state a handler changes. If a handler changed none of"
     " what the page read, running it again would make the same page, so the"
     " browser only calls its canvases' " (code ":draw") " functions again."
     " That's what lets a game move every frame without running the whole page.")
  (p "That's simple, and the browser is fast enough for it to work: a click on"
     " a page of a few hundred elements takes milliseconds. The state that"
     " survives lives outside the program, in a store kept for each page, and"
     " each " (code "define-state") " finds its value there by its position.")
  (note "A web browser does the opposite. It keeps the DOM, a tree of live"
        " objects, and scripts change it in place. Things like focus, scroll"
        " positions and what's typed in a form live on those objects. Here they"
        " have to be kept on the side, by the browser or in page state.")

  (section "Loading pages")
  (p "A location can be one of three things:")
  (entry "pages/hello.sx" "A file, relative to where the browser was started.")
  (entry "browser:chrome" "One of the browser's own pages, from its " (code "ui") " folder.")
  (entry "https://example.com/page.sx" "A page on the web, downloaded over HTTP or"
         " HTTPS. Downloads give up after 10 seconds, or at 4 MB, and a response"
         " that looks like HTML is refused.")
  (p "A download is kept until you leave the page, because the page runs again"
     " on every click and keystroke. F5 or Ctrl+R downloads it again, and starts"
     " its state over.")
  (subsection "The origin rule")
  (p "A page from the web can only reach other pages on the web. It can't link"
     " to, frame, or read the source of a file on your computer, or a "
     (code "browser:") " page. Files and browser pages can reach anything.")

  (p "Links on a web page resolve against the address it was actually served"
     " from, after any redirects.")
  (subsection "Loading in the background")
  (p "In the window, pages and images from the web download on threads of their"
     " own, so a slow server doesn't freeze it. Following a link to a web page"
     " starts the download and leaves the current page up, still usable, with"
     " Loading… in the toolbar. When the download arrives, the thread wakes the"
     " window, and the browser goes there.")
  (p "A page can only run once its source is here, but what it asks for while"
     " it runs may not be: a frame shows Loading… in its place, an image shows"
     " nothing, and " (code "source") " stops the page with Loading… too. Each"
     " starts a download, and when one arrives the page runs again, which is"
     " what it does after every click anyway. Screenshots wait for each download"
     " instead, so they show the finished page.")

  (section "Drawing shapes")
  (p "Layout turns every canvas shape into polygons, in " (code "canvas.rs") ". A"
     " circle has enough sides to look round at its size, a stroke is a rectangle"
     " for each segment and a circle at each joint, and an outline is a ring with a"
     " hole. The painter fills them by the nonzero rule, sampling four lines across"
     " each row of pixels and blending each pixel by how much of it is covered,"
     " which smooths the edges.")
  (p "Selections are kept the same way as find's matches: layout numbers"
     " every character it lays out, in order, so a selection is a range of those"
     " positions, highlighted as the page is laid out.")

  (section "The rest of the source")
  (entry "lisp/builtins.rs, lisp/library.rs" "The builtins, written in Rust. The"
         " prelude, " (code "ui/prelude.sx") ", is the half written in Netscheme:"
         " together they're the equivalent of JavaScript's built-in objects.")
  (entry "lisp/watch.rs" "Notes which state a page reads and which a handler"
         " changes, so the browser can skip running a page that wouldn't change.")
  (entry "lisp/budget.rs" "The limits on steps, nesting and sizes, like a web"
         " browser's script timeouts.")
  (entry "loader.rs" "Locations, downloading, resolving URLs, the origin rule, and"
         " loading pages into frames.")
  (entry "dom.rs" "The tree of elements and text that a page builds.")
  (entry "canvas.rs" "A canvas's shapes as polygons.")
  (entry "damage.rs" "Which parts of the window changed between two frames, so"
         " only those are painted again, the way browsers repaint.")
  (entry "image.rs" "Decoding PNG files, and scaling images.")
  (entry "settings.rs" "Settings kept between runs, like dark mode.")
  (entry "test_server.rs" "A tiny HTTP server, so tests can load pages from the web.")

  (section "What it's built on")
  (p "There's no GUI toolkit, on purpose. Parsing, styling, layout and painting are"
     " the interesting parts of a browser, so Netscheme does them itself. It leans"
     " on a few crates for the rest:")
  (entry "winit" "The window and its input, on Wayland, X11 and elsewhere.")
  (entry "softbuffer" "A plain buffer of pixels to paint into, the way a simple"
         " browser engine paints.")
  (entry "fontdue" "Rasterizes glyphs from the bundled Liberation Sans and Mono."
         " Shaping and kerning are left out for now.")
  (entry "png" "Reads images, and writes screenshots.")
  (entry "arboard" "The clipboard. It uses Wayland's clipboard directly where the"
         " compositor supports it, and X11's otherwise.")
  (entry "ureq, url" "Downloading pages, and resolving addresses.")
  (entry "stacker" "Grows the stack as evaluation needs it, so deep recursion"
         " within the limits doesn't overflow.")
  (p "Rust suits it: it's fast enough to lay out and paint every frame, and its"
     " enums and pattern matching suit an interpreter."))
